1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,982
Maya has had the app open since

2
00:00:02,982 --> 00:00:04,240
breakfast.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,295
On the screen sits a brand-new ticker,

4
00:00:06,295 --> 00:00:06,582
A,

5
00:00:06,582 --> 00:00:06,869
N,

6
00:00:06,869 --> 00:00:07,420
T.

7
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:08,470
Anthropic,

8
00:00:08,470 --> 00:00:11,162
the company behind the Claude A I

9
00:00:11,162 --> 00:00:11,942
models,

10
00:00:11,942 --> 00:00:14,365
sold its first shares to the public

11
00:00:14,365 --> 00:00:16,580
at eighty-three dollars.

12
00:00:16,870 --> 00:00:18,896
And her thumb is hovering over the

13
00:00:18,896 --> 00:00:20,104
buy button.

14
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:22,930
Day one was a roller coaster.

15
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:25,651
The stock opened far above the offer,

16
00:00:25,651 --> 00:00:27,228
slid back all afternoon,

17
00:00:27,228 --> 00:00:30,800
and closed at about ninety-seven dollars.

18
00:00:30,830 --> 00:00:33,040
Everyone on her feed has an opinion.

19
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:35,240
Maya has a rule instead.

20
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:37,484
Never buy something before you've read the

21
00:00:37,484 --> 00:00:38,492
label.

22
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:40,721
So she grabs a clipboard and writes

23
00:00:40,721 --> 00:00:43,510
down the questions a careful buyer asks.

24
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:45,410
What do I actually own?

25
00:00:45,410 --> 00:00:46,590
What am I paying?

26
00:00:46,590 --> 00:00:48,190
When can insiders sell?

27
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:49,990
What will the first report show?

28
00:00:49,990 --> 00:00:51,850
Who does this company depend on?

29
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:53,860
How wild do new stocks get?

30
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:55,470
And who's really in charge?

31
00:00:55,470 --> 00:00:57,145
Let's go through them with her.

32
00:01:02,383 --> 00:01:04,253
A quick note first.

33
00:01:04,253 --> 00:01:06,943
This is not investment advice.

34
00:01:07,003 --> 00:01:07,879
We won't tell you to buy or

35
00:01:07,879 --> 00:01:08,117
sell,

36
00:01:08,117 --> 00:01:09,453
and we have no price target.

37
00:01:09,453 --> 00:01:12,383
Everything here comes from Anthropic's

38
00:01:12,383 --> 00:01:13,422
prospectus,

39
00:01:13,422 --> 00:01:16,086
other public filings and named news

40
00:01:16,086 --> 00:01:16,858
reports,

41
00:01:16,858 --> 00:01:19,323
with our own math labelled.

42
00:01:19,503 --> 00:01:21,378
Our channel uses A I tools,

43
00:01:21,378 --> 00:01:22,410
including Claude,

44
00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:23,863
to make its videos.

45
00:01:23,863 --> 00:01:25,986
Anthropic didn't sponsor or review this

46
00:01:25,986 --> 00:01:26,389
one,

47
00:01:26,389 --> 00:01:28,300
which is exactly why we stick to

48
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:29,537
the filings.

49
00:01:35,183 --> 00:01:36,923
First on the list.

50
00:01:36,923 --> 00:01:38,613
What do you actually own?

51
00:01:38,613 --> 00:01:40,953
Anthropic doesn't have just one kind of

52
00:01:40,953 --> 00:01:41,793
stock.

53
00:01:41,793 --> 00:01:43,563
It has a whole family.

54
00:01:43,563 --> 00:01:45,651
Class A is what trades on the

55
00:01:45,651 --> 00:01:46,408
Nasdaq,

56
00:01:46,408 --> 00:01:48,263
with one vote per share.

57
00:01:48,263 --> 00:01:50,085
Class N gets the same money,

58
00:01:50,085 --> 00:01:50,985
but no vote,

59
00:01:50,985 --> 00:01:53,292
and it's held mostly by big strategic

60
00:01:53,292 --> 00:01:54,313
partners.

61
00:01:54,313 --> 00:01:56,001
And then come the special classes that

62
00:01:56,001 --> 00:01:56,919
carry power,

63
00:01:56,919 --> 00:01:58,645
but no money at all.

64
00:01:58,883 --> 00:02:00,403
Here's the twist.

65
00:02:00,503 --> 00:02:01,716
A single Class F share,

66
00:02:01,716 --> 00:02:03,808
held by a company the co-founders own,

67
00:02:03,808 --> 00:02:05,565
carries just over half of the votes

68
00:02:05,565 --> 00:02:07,923
at every shareholder meeting.

69
00:02:08,493 --> 00:02:09,996
A single Class T share,

70
00:02:09,996 --> 00:02:11,934
held by an independent trust,

71
00:02:11,934 --> 00:02:13,623
picks most of the board.

72
00:02:13,813 --> 00:02:15,392
So a Class A buyer does get

73
00:02:15,392 --> 00:02:16,393
a vote.

74
00:02:16,393 --> 00:02:18,636
But the big decisions are already spoken

75
00:02:18,636 --> 00:02:19,423
for.

76
00:02:19,683 --> 00:02:20,107
Next,

77
00:02:20,107 --> 00:02:22,713
how many shares really exist.

78
00:02:22,853 --> 00:02:24,611
The offering put a big block of

79
00:02:24,611 --> 00:02:26,383
stock into public hands.

80
00:02:26,473 --> 00:02:28,371
But that block is fewer than one

81
00:02:28,371 --> 00:02:31,343
in ten of all the shares outstanding.

82
00:02:31,453 --> 00:02:33,678
Most of the company still belongs to

83
00:02:33,678 --> 00:02:34,991
early investors,

84
00:02:34,991 --> 00:02:36,703
partners and employees.

85
00:02:36,933 --> 00:02:37,961
And for now,

86
00:02:37,961 --> 00:02:39,880
most of them can't sell.

87
00:02:40,117 --> 00:02:41,907
Let me draw the difference between

88
00:02:41,907 --> 00:02:44,297
outstanding and fully diluted.

89
00:02:44,317 --> 00:02:46,340
Outstanding shares are the ones that exist

90
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:47,277
today.

91
00:02:47,377 --> 00:02:49,408
Now add the employee stock options,

92
00:02:49,408 --> 00:02:51,211
the stock awards still vesting,

93
00:02:51,211 --> 00:02:53,492
and the notes Amazon holds that turn

94
00:02:53,492 --> 00:02:54,777
into stock.

95
00:02:54,947 --> 00:02:56,623
That's the fully diluted count,

96
00:02:56,623 --> 00:02:59,107
about a third more shares than today.

97
00:02:59,347 --> 00:03:01,809
So when someone quotes a market value,

98
00:03:01,809 --> 00:03:03,764
ask which count they used.

99
00:03:04,017 --> 00:03:06,637
And that count can keep growing.

100
00:03:06,747 --> 00:03:08,828
Amazon's notes convert a slice at a

101
00:03:08,828 --> 00:03:09,187
time,

102
00:03:09,187 --> 00:03:10,303
every few months,

103
00:03:10,303 --> 00:03:12,227
up to an ownership cap.

104
00:03:12,247 --> 00:03:15,587
Employee plans add new stock every year.

105
00:03:15,737 --> 00:03:17,325
And there's a twist you won't find

106
00:03:17,325 --> 00:03:18,917
at most companies.

107
00:03:19,167 --> 00:03:20,091
Each time the share price hits a

108
00:03:20,091 --> 00:03:20,738
new milestone,

109
00:03:20,738 --> 00:03:21,927
the board may hand extra shares to

110
00:03:21,927 --> 00:03:23,377
Anthropic's own public benefit fund.

111
00:03:23,377 --> 00:03:27,057
Good for its mission.

112
00:03:27,057 --> 00:03:28,014
A slightly smaller slice for everyone

113
00:03:28,014 --> 00:03:28,710
else.

114
00:03:28,950 --> 00:03:30,800
So that's what you own.

115
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:31,345
Now,

116
00:03:31,345 --> 00:03:33,668
what are you paying for it?

117
00:03:39,317 --> 00:03:41,527
Next on the list.

118
00:03:41,667 --> 00:03:42,747
The price.

119
00:03:42,747 --> 00:03:45,705
Anthropic barely makes an operating profit

120
00:03:45,705 --> 00:03:46,080
yet,

121
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:48,053
so the usual price to earnings ratio

122
00:03:48,053 --> 00:03:49,047
doesn't work.

123
00:03:49,157 --> 00:03:52,167
Investors use price to sales instead.

124
00:03:52,417 --> 00:03:53,652
Take the latest close,

125
00:03:53,652 --> 00:03:55,934
multiply by the shares outstanding,

126
00:03:55,934 --> 00:03:57,608
and divide by the last year of

127
00:03:57,608 --> 00:03:58,627
revenue.

128
00:03:58,787 --> 00:03:59,623
By our math,

129
00:03:59,623 --> 00:04:02,365
that's about thirty-six times sales.

130
00:04:02,617 --> 00:04:03,991
Let me put that next to the

131
00:04:03,991 --> 00:04:04,577
giants.

132
00:04:04,577 --> 00:04:05,747
Here's Nvidia.

133
00:04:05,747 --> 00:04:07,147
Here's Microsoft.

134
00:04:07,147 --> 00:04:08,446
On the same day's close,

135
00:04:08,446 --> 00:04:09,525
with the same math,

136
00:04:09,525 --> 00:04:11,507
both sit at about ten times their

137
00:04:11,507 --> 00:04:12,407
sales.

138
00:04:12,407 --> 00:04:13,354
And here's A,

139
00:04:13,354 --> 00:04:13,721
N,

140
00:04:13,721 --> 00:04:14,088
T,

141
00:04:14,088 --> 00:04:15,697
towering over them.

142
00:04:15,697 --> 00:04:17,047
Same yardstick.

143
00:04:17,047 --> 00:04:18,845
Very different height.

144
00:04:19,083 --> 00:04:20,633
Is that crazy?

145
00:04:20,693 --> 00:04:22,433
Not automatically.

146
00:04:22,533 --> 00:04:24,261
The giants grow far more slowly,

147
00:04:24,261 --> 00:04:26,448
and Anthropic's revenue so far this year

148
00:04:26,448 --> 00:04:28,417
is many times what it was a

149
00:04:28,417 --> 00:04:29,673
year earlier.

150
00:04:30,013 --> 00:04:32,172
Measured against its latest monthly pace,

151
00:04:32,172 --> 00:04:33,423
stretched over a year,

152
00:04:33,423 --> 00:04:35,293
the multiple shrinks a lot.

153
00:04:35,413 --> 00:04:37,093
That's the bull case.

154
00:04:37,303 --> 00:04:39,043
The bear case is simple.

155
00:04:39,173 --> 00:04:41,172
A price that high only works if

156
00:04:41,172 --> 00:04:42,646
fast growth lasts for years,

157
00:04:42,646 --> 00:04:45,745
and fast growth rarely lasts forever.

158
00:04:45,983 --> 00:04:47,983
One more label to check.

159
00:04:47,983 --> 00:04:50,162
Anthropic holds a big pile of cash

160
00:04:50,162 --> 00:04:51,448
from its private rounds,

161
00:04:51,448 --> 00:04:53,605
plus the money from this offering,

162
00:04:53,605 --> 00:04:55,513
and very little debt.

163
00:04:55,603 --> 00:04:57,022
So its enterprise value,

164
00:04:57,022 --> 00:04:59,058
the price of the business itself,

165
00:04:59,058 --> 00:05:01,303
is lower than its market value.

166
00:05:01,333 --> 00:05:03,863
Our multiple uses market value.

167
00:05:03,863 --> 00:05:05,602
Whatever headline you read,

168
00:05:05,602 --> 00:05:07,522
check which one it uses.

169
00:05:13,183 --> 00:05:14,433
Next question.

170
00:05:14,433 --> 00:05:16,333
When can insiders sell?

171
00:05:16,333 --> 00:05:18,953
Most new stocks come with a lock-up.

172
00:05:18,953 --> 00:05:20,945
Early holders promise not to sell for

173
00:05:20,945 --> 00:05:22,103
a while.

174
00:05:22,103 --> 00:05:24,373
Anthropic's isn't one cliff.

175
00:05:24,373 --> 00:05:25,733
It's a staircase.

176
00:05:25,733 --> 00:05:27,848
Some employee shares could trade from day

177
00:05:27,848 --> 00:05:28,593
one.

178
00:05:28,593 --> 00:05:30,825
More unlock in steps through December,

179
00:05:30,825 --> 00:05:32,863
January and February.

180
00:05:32,863 --> 00:05:34,877
And a bigger wave follows right after

181
00:05:34,877 --> 00:05:36,685
the first annual results.

182
00:05:36,950 --> 00:05:38,499
Then comes the big gate,

183
00:05:38,499 --> 00:05:40,810
the one most investors will circle.

184
00:05:40,810 --> 00:05:42,984
It opens right after the results for

185
00:05:42,984 --> 00:05:44,266
January to March,

186
00:05:44,266 --> 00:05:46,222
or at the very latest on May

187
00:05:46,222 --> 00:05:47,909
seventeenth next year.

188
00:05:48,150 --> 00:05:49,989
Behind that gate sit up to three

189
00:05:49,989 --> 00:05:51,116
billion shares,

190
00:05:51,116 --> 00:05:53,415
the biggest single step on the whole

191
00:05:53,415 --> 00:05:54,705
calendar.

192
00:05:54,950 --> 00:05:56,027
The co-founders,

193
00:05:56,027 --> 00:05:58,444
and investors holding more than a sliver

194
00:05:58,444 --> 00:05:59,741
of the company,

195
00:05:59,741 --> 00:06:00,900
wait longer.

196
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:02,218
Up to a full year,

197
00:06:02,218 --> 00:06:04,452
until the results for next summer are

198
00:06:04,452 --> 00:06:04,876
out,

199
00:06:04,876 --> 00:06:08,157
or the following November at the latest.

200
00:06:08,417 --> 00:06:10,927
Unlocked doesn't mean sold.

201
00:06:11,087 --> 00:06:13,077
Plenty of holders keep their shares.

202
00:06:13,077 --> 00:06:14,329
But more shares that can be sold

203
00:06:14,329 --> 00:06:15,565
means more possible sellers,

204
00:06:15,565 --> 00:06:17,307
so Maya writes these dates down.

205
00:06:17,307 --> 00:06:19,117
One more detail.

206
00:06:19,117 --> 00:06:21,357
The chip and cloud companies that bought

207
00:06:21,357 --> 00:06:24,593
shares privately at the offer price aren't

208
00:06:24,593 --> 00:06:26,687
in the lock-up at all.

209
00:06:26,787 --> 00:06:28,422
But their shares are restricted,

210
00:06:28,422 --> 00:06:29,891
so they can't simply sell them on

211
00:06:29,891 --> 00:06:31,960
the market whenever they like.

212
00:06:37,617 --> 00:06:38,217
Next,

213
00:06:38,217 --> 00:06:40,397
the first report card.

214
00:06:40,677 --> 00:06:42,802
Anthropic's first results as a public

215
00:06:42,802 --> 00:06:44,905
company will cover this full year,

216
00:06:44,905 --> 00:06:47,417
and they should arrive early next year.

217
00:06:47,697 --> 00:06:49,817
The date hasn't been announced yet.

218
00:06:49,877 --> 00:06:51,547
But it matters twice.

219
00:06:51,767 --> 00:06:53,062
It's the first fresh look at the

220
00:06:53,062 --> 00:06:54,867
numbers since the offering.

221
00:06:54,867 --> 00:06:56,447
And it's also the trigger for one

222
00:06:56,447 --> 00:06:58,101
of those lock-up waves.

223
00:06:58,350 --> 00:07:00,930
Here's what Maya will look for.

224
00:07:01,270 --> 00:07:01,437
First,

225
00:07:01,437 --> 00:07:02,170
revenue growth.

226
00:07:02,170 --> 00:07:03,480
Is the rocket still climbing?

227
00:07:03,480 --> 00:07:04,056
Second,

228
00:07:04,056 --> 00:07:05,450
the compute bill.

229
00:07:05,620 --> 00:07:08,680
How much do chips and data centers

230
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:12,000
cost for each dollar of sales?

231
00:07:12,090 --> 00:07:12,169
Third,

232
00:07:12,169 --> 00:07:12,840
operating cash flow.

233
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:16,440
Is real cash still coming in?

234
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:16,982
And fourth,

235
00:07:16,982 --> 00:07:18,750
the commitment schedule.

236
00:07:18,750 --> 00:07:20,762
How much has it promised to spend,

237
00:07:20,762 --> 00:07:21,769
and when?

238
00:07:22,017 --> 00:07:24,319
That compute bill is the one to

239
00:07:24,319 --> 00:07:24,977
watch.

240
00:07:24,977 --> 00:07:25,475
Last year,

241
00:07:25,475 --> 00:07:27,022
it was bigger than the sales it

242
00:07:27,022 --> 00:07:27,717
helped earn.

243
00:07:27,717 --> 00:07:28,734
So far this year,

244
00:07:28,734 --> 00:07:31,017
it's down to about fifty-seven cents for

245
00:07:31,017 --> 00:07:33,277
every dollar of revenue.

246
00:07:33,317 --> 00:07:34,567
If that keeps falling,

247
00:07:34,567 --> 00:07:36,227
margins open up.

248
00:07:36,227 --> 00:07:37,270
If it climbs back,

249
00:07:37,270 --> 00:07:39,147
the story gets harder.

250
00:07:39,383 --> 00:07:41,733
And then there are the promises.

251
00:07:41,733 --> 00:07:43,856
Anthropic has signed up to spend about

252
00:07:43,856 --> 00:07:45,978
nine hundred billion dollars on computing

253
00:07:45,978 --> 00:07:48,082
and data centers over many years,

254
00:07:48,082 --> 00:07:50,204
much of it owed whether customers show

255
00:07:50,204 --> 00:07:51,383
up or not.

256
00:07:51,533 --> 00:07:52,729
On the other side,

257
00:07:52,729 --> 00:07:54,331
it has a large cash cushion,

258
00:07:54,331 --> 00:07:56,359
and its operating cash flow turned

259
00:07:56,359 --> 00:07:57,973
positive this year.

260
00:07:58,093 --> 00:08:00,264
The report will show whether that cash

261
00:08:00,264 --> 00:08:02,070
keeps pace with the bills.

262
00:08:07,717 --> 00:08:08,797
Next.

263
00:08:08,797 --> 00:08:10,957
Who does Anthropic depend on?

264
00:08:10,957 --> 00:08:13,277
Start with Amazon and Google.

265
00:08:13,277 --> 00:08:15,417
Both are big shareholders.

266
00:08:15,417 --> 00:08:17,475
Both rent it the computing power it

267
00:08:17,475 --> 00:08:18,487
runs on.

268
00:08:18,617 --> 00:08:20,353
Both sell Claude to their own cloud

269
00:08:20,353 --> 00:08:21,697
customers.

270
00:08:21,737 --> 00:08:23,481
And both build A I models of

271
00:08:23,481 --> 00:08:24,517
their own.

272
00:08:24,547 --> 00:08:25,217
Partner,

273
00:08:25,217 --> 00:08:25,887
landlord,

274
00:08:25,887 --> 00:08:27,482
salesman and rival,

275
00:08:27,482 --> 00:08:28,775
all at once.

276
00:08:29,017 --> 00:08:30,847
Let me draw how that works.

277
00:08:30,847 --> 00:08:32,927
Amazon invests in Anthropic.

278
00:08:32,927 --> 00:08:35,637
Anthropic pays Amazon for computing.

279
00:08:35,637 --> 00:08:37,927
Amazon's cloud customers buy Claude,

280
00:08:37,927 --> 00:08:40,147
and Amazon keeps a cut.

281
00:08:40,397 --> 00:08:42,857
Google sits in a similar triangle.

282
00:08:42,877 --> 00:08:44,832
The prospectus itself warns that these

283
00:08:44,832 --> 00:08:46,787
ties could create conflicts of interest

284
00:08:46,787 --> 00:08:49,559
and affect its access to computing.

285
00:08:49,817 --> 00:08:52,107
The reassuring part is in the chips.

286
00:08:52,107 --> 00:08:54,169
Anthropic trains and runs Claude on chips

287
00:08:54,169 --> 00:08:55,291
from Amazon,

288
00:08:55,291 --> 00:08:55,956
Google,

289
00:08:55,956 --> 00:08:57,536
Nvidia and A M D,

290
00:08:57,536 --> 00:08:59,598
so no single supplier can choke it

291
00:08:59,598 --> 00:09:00,327
off.

292
00:09:00,327 --> 00:09:01,307
The flip side?

293
00:09:01,307 --> 00:09:03,283
Several of those suppliers are also

294
00:09:03,283 --> 00:09:04,252
shareholders,

295
00:09:04,252 --> 00:09:06,631
and its commitments to them are huge.

296
00:09:06,883 --> 00:09:08,513
And the customers?

297
00:09:08,513 --> 00:09:09,720
So far this year,

298
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:12,429
no single customer was big enough to

299
00:09:12,429 --> 00:09:14,403
be named in the filing.

300
00:09:14,403 --> 00:09:14,689
Last year,

301
00:09:14,689 --> 00:09:15,683
a couple were.

302
00:09:16,033 --> 00:09:17,883
That's a healthier spread.

303
00:09:18,103 --> 00:09:19,852
But a big share of sales still

304
00:09:19,852 --> 00:09:21,819
flows through the cloud marketplaces of

305
00:09:21,819 --> 00:09:23,163
those same partners.

306
00:09:23,163 --> 00:09:25,066
So the web of ties runs both

307
00:09:25,066 --> 00:09:25,938
ways.

308
00:09:31,583 --> 00:09:32,833
Next.

309
00:09:32,843 --> 00:09:35,273
How wild can a new stock get?

310
00:09:35,413 --> 00:09:37,583
Look back at Facebook's debut.

311
00:09:37,583 --> 00:09:38,738
Within a few months,

312
00:09:38,738 --> 00:09:40,696
its shares had fallen to about half

313
00:09:40,696 --> 00:09:41,656
the offer price,

314
00:09:41,656 --> 00:09:43,418
and the first lock-up ended right in

315
00:09:43,418 --> 00:09:45,193
the middle of that slide.

316
00:09:45,213 --> 00:09:46,855
A year after the offering,

317
00:09:46,855 --> 00:09:48,931
it was still below the offer.

318
00:09:49,183 --> 00:09:51,233
Now the opposite story.

319
00:09:51,263 --> 00:09:53,596
Chip designer Arm listed in the fall,

320
00:09:53,596 --> 00:09:54,859
popped on day one,

321
00:09:54,859 --> 00:09:57,743
then slipped below its offer within weeks.

322
00:09:57,743 --> 00:09:58,614
Months later,

323
00:09:58,614 --> 00:10:00,824
a strong quarterly report came out,

324
00:10:00,824 --> 00:10:02,833
and the stock jumped by nearly half

325
00:10:02,833 --> 00:10:04,549
in a single day.

326
00:10:04,783 --> 00:10:06,783
Same lesson from both.

327
00:10:06,783 --> 00:10:07,807
In its first year,

328
00:10:07,807 --> 00:10:09,896
a new stock trades on very little

329
00:10:09,896 --> 00:10:11,093
history.

330
00:10:11,093 --> 00:10:12,673
In both of these cases,

331
00:10:12,673 --> 00:10:14,962
some of the biggest moves came around

332
00:10:14,962 --> 00:10:17,503
lock-up dates and earnings reports.

333
00:10:17,503 --> 00:10:19,641
Those are exactly the dates on Maya's

334
00:10:19,641 --> 00:10:20,669
clipboard.

335
00:10:26,317 --> 00:10:27,507
Last question.

336
00:10:27,507 --> 00:10:29,117
Who governs this company?

337
00:10:29,117 --> 00:10:32,157
Anthropic is a public benefit corporation.

338
00:10:32,157 --> 00:10:32,712
By law,

339
00:10:32,712 --> 00:10:34,819
its board must balance shareholders' money

340
00:10:34,819 --> 00:10:37,118
with the interests of people affected by

341
00:10:37,118 --> 00:10:37,673
its work,

342
00:10:37,673 --> 00:10:39,187
and with a stated mission,

343
00:10:39,187 --> 00:10:41,868
the responsible development of advanced A

344
00:10:41,868 --> 00:10:44,958
I for the long-term benefit of humanity.

345
00:10:45,217 --> 00:10:47,371
On top of that sits the Long-Term

346
00:10:47,371 --> 00:10:48,690
Benefit Trust,

347
00:10:48,690 --> 00:10:50,844
a small group of trustees with no

348
00:10:50,844 --> 00:10:53,867
financial stake in the company.

349
00:10:54,267 --> 00:10:54,798
Through that Class T share,

350
00:10:54,798 --> 00:10:55,807
they elect most of the board.

351
00:10:55,807 --> 00:10:58,601
That makes Anthropic what Nasdaq calls a

352
00:10:58,601 --> 00:11:00,354
controlled company,

353
00:11:00,354 --> 00:11:02,387
which may skip some of the usual

354
00:11:02,387 --> 00:11:04,257
governance rules.

355
00:11:04,517 --> 00:11:06,477
What does that mean for a buyer?

356
00:11:06,507 --> 00:11:08,294
Supporters say it shields long-term

357
00:11:08,294 --> 00:11:10,457
thinking from short-term pressure.

358
00:11:10,497 --> 00:11:12,288
The prospectus itself warns that the trust

359
00:11:12,288 --> 00:11:14,239
may have interests that differ from yours,

360
00:11:14,239 --> 00:11:15,672
and could use its power in ways

361
00:11:15,672 --> 00:11:17,167
you disagree with.

362
00:11:17,307 --> 00:11:18,004
Either way,

363
00:11:18,004 --> 00:11:18,701
if you buy,

364
00:11:18,701 --> 00:11:20,488
you're joining a company where your vote

365
00:11:20,488 --> 00:11:21,624
has limits.

366
00:11:27,283 --> 00:11:27,742
So,

367
00:11:27,742 --> 00:11:29,793
what would change the picture?

368
00:11:29,883 --> 00:11:30,908
On the bright side,

369
00:11:30,908 --> 00:11:32,980
watch for revenue that keeps growing fast,

370
00:11:32,980 --> 00:11:34,863
a compute bill that keeps shrinking for

371
00:11:34,863 --> 00:11:36,725
every dollar of sales,

372
00:11:36,725 --> 00:11:38,378
cash flow that stays positive,

373
00:11:38,378 --> 00:11:40,471
and lock-up waves that pass without a

374
00:11:40,471 --> 00:11:41,908
flood of selling.

375
00:11:42,150 --> 00:11:43,264
On the dark side,

376
00:11:43,264 --> 00:11:45,060
watch for growth that slows sharply,

377
00:11:45,060 --> 00:11:47,084
a compute bill that climbs back up,

378
00:11:47,084 --> 00:11:48,675
cash that can't keep pace with the

379
00:11:48,675 --> 00:11:49,562
commitments,

380
00:11:49,562 --> 00:11:51,836
or big holders heading for the exit

381
00:11:51,836 --> 00:11:53,800
when the gates open.

382
00:11:54,050 --> 00:11:57,200
Maya pins the clipboard to her fridge.

383
00:11:57,250 --> 00:11:58,760
She didn't decide today,

384
00:11:58,760 --> 00:12:01,110
and we're not deciding for her.

385
00:12:01,370 --> 00:12:03,148
But now she knows what she would

386
00:12:03,148 --> 00:12:03,631
own,

387
00:12:03,631 --> 00:12:04,876
what she would pay,

388
00:12:04,876 --> 00:12:07,000
and which dates to watch.

389
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:09,004
The prices in this video are from

390
00:12:09,004 --> 00:12:09,975
the first day's close,

391
00:12:09,975 --> 00:12:12,155
and every figure is sourced in the

392
00:12:12,155 --> 00:12:13,350
description.

393
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:14,562
And once more,

394
00:12:14,562 --> 00:12:17,219
this is not investment advice.

395
00:12:17,483 --> 00:12:18,940
Next in the series,

